  ],
  "text": "The East India Company had its own army, and it was bigger than Britain's. It started as a trading licence. Buy over there, sell over here. But trading needs warehouses, and warehouses need guards. Guards need a fort, and a fort needs land, and land needs an arrangement with whoever holds it. Each step is reasonable. The end of the chain is not. After Plassey in seventeen fifty seven it won the right to collect tax across Bengal. So a company was now funding its own army out of the taxes of the people it governed. It ran courts. It set prices. It decided what got planted. The pattern repeats elsewhere. Commercial access hardens into control, then into extraction. Nobody voted for it. It was a supply chain that kept needing one more thing to protect itself. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
